  "Source": "K. H. Behrndt and D. W. Doughty, \"Fabrication of Multilayer Dielectric Films,\" J. Vac. Sci. Technol. 3, 264-272 (1966). doi:10.1116/1.1492487 - Sec. II.C p. 271 text, Eq. (1) p. 269, Fig. 6 and Table I p. 272",
  "Notes": "9-layer quarter-wave ZnS/Na3AlF6 dielectric laser mirror (high reflector near 6328 A)\nSpecification: Maximum reflectance at the design wavelength (quarter-wave high reflector).\nReported: Table I p. 272: theoretical 1 - Rmax = 1.65 % from Eq. (1) (nH = 2.32, nL = 1.37, ns = 1.52, air incidence; recomputed here from Eq. (1): 1.656 %); experimental minimum transmittance Tmax = 1.60 % (+/- 0.02 %, read from Fig. 6). The authors conclude absorption is negligible since T ~ 1 - R. Fig. 6 shows the experimental T vs wavelength curves for all four 9-layer combinations (6000-7000 A axis).\nNotes: Quarter-wave stack (H L)^m H: the paper says the first layer deposited on the substrate is the high-index material and the last layer is also high index, every layer a quarter wave (QWOT 1) at the design wavelength (p. 264-266). The stack is symmetric, so the paper's substrate-first order and the incident-first order listed here are the same. Light is incident from air on the coated side (reflectance monitored from the front; Eq. 1 is written for air incidence). Indices are the constant values the authors used for their calculations (p. 269, 271, Table I); no dispersion or absorption. Substrate is soda-lime glass, ns = 1.52 (p. 269); Eq. (1) ignores the uncoated back face of the substrate, so compare 1-Rmax with a single-interface calculation (no back-surface reflection). reference_nm 632.8 is an assumption: the paper monitors with a 6328 A He-Ne laser and states the mirrors have their maximum at 6328 A for the standard monitor position (p. 268), but the plotted transmittance minima (Figs. 4-6) look centred nearer ~640 nm (read off the plots, approximate). Normal incidence assumed (spectrophotometer measurements). Cryolite: the literature value 1.33 is quoted but the authors found n = 1.37 experimentally and used it (p. 271).\nLayers from Sec. II.C p. 271 text, Eq. (1) p. 269, Fig. 6 and Table I p. 272; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours 1 - R 1.66 % at 632.8 nm; paper 1 - Rmax 1.65 % (Eq. 1).",
